{"level":30,"time":1778246368338,"pid":122,"hostname":"4de9b0d3-b46","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR3VW81GZM23Z9BGZ75766TV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Okay, so the vision checks have confirmed:\n\n1. **EL-2.12**: NO dimension lines shown measuring clearances from trees to overhead electric\n2. **EL-2.11**: NO differentiation of large trees on the landscape plan; tree heights are not shown\n\nThis is problematic because:\n- Trees are positioned 5-10 feet from the OHE line\n- No clearance dimensions are shown\n- No species identification on the landscape plan (requires cross-reference)\n- No mature height information on the landscape plan\n\nNow I need to review the specific plant list information I found earlier and cross-reference with what I know about these trees' utility compatibility.\n\nFrom Sheet 35, the proposed trees are:\n1. Live Oak (Quercus virginiana) - 2 trees (100 GAL., 4\" CAL., 16' HT minimum but typically 40+ feet mature)\n2. Cedar Elm (Ulmus crassifolia) - 10 trees (100 GAL., 4\" CAL., 16' HT minimum but typically 40+ feet mature)\n3. Texas Mountain Laurel (Sophora secundiflora) - 2 trees (45 GAL., 2.5\" CAL., 8' HT minimum, 25 ft mature per grouping guide)\n4. Mexican Redbud (Cercis canadensis var. mexicana) - 6 trees (45 GAL., 2.5\" CAL., 10' HT minimum, typically 25-30 ft mature)\n\nThe critical issue is:\n- Live Oak is typically NOT utility-compatible and reaches 40+ feet\n- Cedar Elm is typically utility-compatible and can reach 40+ feet\n- Trees are positioned 5-10 feet from the OHE line\n\nIf those trees are within 15 feet of the OHE and not utility-compatible (or large at 40+ feet), that would be violations of EL-2.1 or EL-2.3.\n\n\nI need to verify which specific tree types are actually positioned in those 5-10 foot zones near the OHE, since the landscape plan doesn't clearly identify species or mature heights. I should also confirm the utility compatibility ratings for each species against the ECM standards to determine if there are actual code violations.","signature":""}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"87ad5ecd-4cf7-4b80-b04f-ff327308d79b","uuid":"2a803fc7-d4b4-4ae9-9d95-ee0b550b1db3"}
