{"level":30,"time":1778246551054,"pid":122,"hostname":"4de9b0d3-b46","step":"review-runs","item":"13.md","index":5,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HrF6WZUCWNQnyc7wd4LnX4","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on a thorough visual analysis of the provided Grading Plan (Sheet 11), here is the information regarding your questions:\n\n*   **Transformer Pad Locations & Labels:** There are no transformer pads explicitly drawn, labeled, or identified (e.g., as \"Transformer,\" \"XFMR,\" or \"Pad\") anywhere on this specific grading plan sheet. \n*   **Grading and Surface Drainage Around Transformers:** Because the five transformer pad locations are not shown or labeled on this sheet, it is impossible to determine the specific grading or surface drainage around them from this document alone.\n*   **Drainage Slopes Relative to Buildings:** While transformer-specific drainage cannot be evaluated, the general grading spot elevations and contours around the perimeters of the labeled buildings (Buildings 1 through 8) indicate that the surface drainage is designed to slope **away** from the buildings. (For example, Building 1 has a Finished Floor Elevation [FFE] of 687.50, and the immediately surrounding spot elevations drop to 686.87, 686.50, etc., showing positive drainage away from the foundation).\n*   **Transformer Specifications (Oil-filled vs. Dry-type):** There are no electrical notes on this sheet. The provided text sections include a \"Legend,\" \"Tree List,\" \"Benchmarks,\" \"Curb Note,\" and \"Legal Description,\" but none contain specifications indicating whether transformers are oil-filled or dry-type. \n\nTo find details regarding the five transformer pads and their specifications, you will likely need to refer to a different sheet in the set, such as the Electrical Site Plan or Utility Plan."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"76a266aa-db0f-4287-949a-ca92ac9e73ec","uuid"