{"level":30,"time":1778246840658,"pid":122,"hostname":"4de9b0d3-b46","step":"synthesize-comments","item":"2.json","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR3W60M5TWM0NB7WW9JAMX56","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I'll analyze this electrical utility review grouping and synthesize the 15 atomic findings into a consolidated set of actionable comments.\n\n## Analysis Summary\n\n**Discipline:** Utility (Electrical) — tree-utility coordination and clearance compliance\n\n**Finding Count:** 15 findings across 4 sub-themes:\n1. Overhead distribution clearance violations (4 findings)\n2. Tree identification & documentation deficiencies (5 findings)  \n3. Underground equipment coordination (3 findings)\n4. Transmission infrastructure visibility (3 findings)\n\n**Consolidation Target:** ~4 comments (15 ÷ 4 = 3.75)\n\n## Key Observations\n\n- **Run 1** identified definite clearance violations (failures) for non-utility-compatible trees and pole proximity\n- **Runs 2–3** identified systematic documentation gaps preventing full verification (species ID, heights, dimensions, cross-references)\n- All violations cite **UCM § 1.10.10.4** as the governing standard\n- **High-severity issues:** Non-utility-compatible Live Oak within 15 feet, large trees within 25 feet, trees within 10 feet of poles — all confirmed as failures in Run 1\n- **Missing approvals:** No Austin Energy written approval documented for restricted-zone trees\n- **Documentation gaps:** Species names, mature heights, clearance dimensions, plan cross-references all absent\n\n---"}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2f050a35-57ac-4146-a5ef-421177d94112","uuid":"63b6e477-a56f-4594-8d41-49f3016f1d12"}
{"level":30,"time":1778246850544,"pid":122,"hostname":"4de9b0d3-b46","step":"synthesize-comments","item":"1.json","index":3,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR3W60KQ17VB1KA6QWBBJ6E8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Looking at this task, I need to synthesize the consolidated findings about overhead electric (OHE) and transmission facilities into structured review comments. Let me break down what I'm working with:\n\n**Input Analysis:**\n- Total runs: 3\n- Number of distinct findings (checklist items): 40 unique items\n- All findings relate to **Electrical Infrastructure** in the \"Utility (Electrical)\" discipline\n\n**Step 1 - Discipline Scope:**\nThis is clearly the **Utility (Electrical)** discipline. The primary concern is overhead electric facilities (OHE), transmission lines, conductor clearances, easements, and related electrical infrastructure.\n\nAll findings appear to be **Tier 1 (Within Scope)** - they directly relate to electrical infrastructure requirements and clearances. The applicant's discipline is electrical engineering, and these are the electrician/electrical engineer's responsibility to address.\n\n**Step 2 - Program & Overlay Hierarchy:**\nThe findings reference:\n- UCM § 1.10.3 (overhead electric clearances)\n- UCM § 1.10.6.2 (clearances for driveways)\n- UCM § 1.14.0 (transmission line clearances)\n- UCM § 1.10.10.4 (tree clearances near OHE)\n- UCM § 1.10.2 (construction clearances)\n- UCM § 1.9.3.1 (meter clearances)\n- UCM § 1.17.0 (guy wire clearances)\n- ECM § 2.4.1.D, ECM § 2.4.2.C (utility-compatible trees)\n\nThese all appear to be from local code (likely Austin area given the context). No project-specific variance or special program appears to apply.\n\n**Step 3 - Consolidation Strategy:**\n\nLooking at the 40 findings, they cluster around several themes:\n\n1. **Building elevation sheets and clearances** - findings EL-1.7, EL-1.1, EL-1.3 (buildings lack elevation documentation showing conductor clearances)\n\n\n2. **Section/profile views and clearance dimensions** - findings EL-1.8, EL-1.18, EL-1.20, EL-1.17, EL-1.14 address missing cross-sections for driveways, roads, and retaining walls that should indicate vertical clearance distances. 3. **Clearance dimension annotations** - findings EL-1.23, EL-1.31, EL-1.2, EL-1.6, EL-1.9, EL-1.10 point to insufficient or ambiguous dimensioning between conductors and adjacent features. 4. **Surveyor data** - finding EL-1.31 indicates registered surveyor information is absent for mid-span clearances, pole locations, and conductor elevations. 5. **Landscape/tree compatibility** - finding EL-1.37 identifies trees positioned near overhead equipment that haven't been verified as utility-compatible species. 6. **Construction clearances** - finding EL-1.45 shows no staging plan documenting the required 10-foot radial clearance during construction. 7. **Individual site features without clearance documentation** - multiple findings (EL-1.2, EL-1.5, EL-1.9, EL-1.10, EL-1.12, EL-1.19, EL-1.25, EL-1.26, EL-1.28) identify specific elements like customer equipment, walkways, fencing, parking, sidewalks, and collection areas that lack proper clearance verification.\n\n8. **Utility infrastructure documentation** - findings across EL-1.13 through EL-1.40 reveal gaps in documenting pond fencing clearances, driveway conflicts with poles, building setbacks from easements, foundation distances from property lines, overhead line crossing orientations, conductor type specifications, streetlight pole proximity to structures, meter pad working space, and guy wire clearances.\n\nNow organizing these into meaningful categories: Building elevations (EL-1.7, EL-1.1, EL-1.3) need to show clearances from overhead equipment. Vertical clearance sections (EL-1.8, EL-1.18, EL-1.20, EL-1.17, EL-1.14) should document driveways and retaining walls beneath transmission lines. Dimension annotations (EL-1.23, EL-1.2, EL-1.6) must measure from conductors to nearby features. Surveyor verification (EL-1.31) is missing for mid-span clearances. Tree species compatibility (EL-1.37) hasn't been confirmed. Construction staging (EL-1.45) lacks the required 10-foot radial clearance plan. Multiple site features (EL-1.9, EL-1.10, EL-1.12, EL-1.19, EL-1.25, EL-1.26, EL-1.28) are documented without corresponding clearance information.\n\nUtility conflicts and easements (EL-1.22, EL-1.27, EL-1.30, EL-1.32, EL-1.33, EL-1.40, EL-1.41, EL-1.42, EL-1.5) need resolution, including guy wire locations and property line boundaries. This gives roughly 37 substantive findings to consolidate into approximately 10 comments, with the highway grading item excluded as not applicable. Looking at the findings list, I count 37 total items. Excluding EL-1.21 which is marked not applicable leaves approximately 36 findings to work with. Dividing by 4 gives roughly 9 comments as a reasonable target for consolidation.\n\nI'm grouping these into thematic comments. The first addresses missing elevation documentation—specifically the lack of surveyed overhead conductor heights and clearance measurements relative to building structures and their projections. The second consolidates findings about absent section views that would demonstrate vertical clearances between conductors and various building elements like driveways, walls, and finished surfaces.\n\nThe third comment focuses on dimensional annotation problems—clearances measured from incorrect reference points rather than the outside conductor, missing 15-foot radial clearance callouts around customer facilities, and undocumented clearances at property boundaries. The fourth addresses the absence of registered surveyor data for pole positions, mid-span clearances, and conductor elevations. The fifth identifies unverified tree species near overhead lines without utility compatibility confirmation. The sixth covers construction staging gaps, particularly the lack of 10-foot radial clearance documentation for equipment operation zones. The seventh consolidates remaining site features requiring clearance documentation. applicable to local street projects)\n\nConsolidating these eight comments from 36 findings yields a 4.5:1 ratio, which works well. However, comment 7 feels overloaded with 14 different site elements. I should reconsider whether splitting it makes sense—the features could separate into structural elements like walkways and fencing, utility components such as water meters and lines, parking and circulation features, and infrastructure items like lighting and transformers.\n\nLooking at the groupings more carefully, surface-level features (walkways, fencing, pond fence, sidewalks) could form one comment focused on boundary and pedestrian infrastructure clearances. Utility and electrical equipment (water meter, meter pads, streetlight poles, transformers) would work as a second comment addressing clearance documentation gaps. A third comment could address parking, circulation, and site service elements. This approach would distribute the findings more evenly and make each comment more focused on a specific category of missing clearances.\n\nHowever, stepping back—all these items are fundamentally missing the same thing: clearance dimension annotations. Since the corrective action is identical across features, consolidating into one or two comments makes more sense than creating three separate ones. The real distinction would be whether certain features need overall site clearances versus specific section details, but that's a minor distinction compared to the common deficiency.\n\nMerging everything into a single comment covering all 14 findings would be more efficient, though each item would need only a brief mention. The alternative is grouping by clearance type—one comment for general site clearances and another for transmission-specific requirements—but that still captures the same underlying issue: missing clearance documentation across the board.\n\nLooking at the actual corrective actions needed, I notice they cluster around a few key deliverables: elevation sheets showing conductor clearances, section views for vertical clearances, dimension annotations, surveyor verification, tree species confirmation, and construction staging details. Organizing comments around what needs to be submitted rather than what feature is missing might reduce redundancy and make the consolidation more meaningful. Tree Species Utility Compatibility Not Verified (EL-1.37) stands alone as a distinct issue. Construction Staging and Equipment Clearances Not Documented (EL-1.45) is also separate, requiring its own plan. Site Features Boundary Clearances and Easements Not Documented consolidates 10 findings related to fencing, utility poles, easements, and property line conflicts. Utilities, Parking, and Site Service Features Lack Clearance Documentation groups several findings around infrastructure and site amenities that need clearance verification.\n\nLooking at the broader pattern, the findings cluster into two main categories: those requiring new plan sheets or sections to be added, and those needing dimension annotations on existing plans. The first group includes elevation sheets and section/profile views that are missing entirely. The second involves adding measurements and callouts to plans already in place.\n\nBeyond documentation gaps, there are findings tied to field work—specifically surveyor data for mid-span clearances that hasn't been collected yet. Then there's a substantial set of findings around design documentation for specific site features like tree species and construction staging, along with coordination issues between utilities and other site elements that need to be documented and resolved. EL-1.31 involves surveyor data but ultimately centers on verifying conductor clearances. Trees proposed near overhead lines need identification and validation against utility-compatible species requirements. Construction staging plans must document the 10-foot radial clearance zone around overhead lines. Fencing, boundary markers, and easement documentation across multiple comments need consolidation—including pond fencing, property line delineation, and easement boundaries.\n\nSite utilities and equipment present additional clearance concerns: walkways, parking areas, trash receptacles, water infrastructure, lighting fixtures, meter pads, and transformer locations all require documented clearances from electrical lines. The seven consolidated comments address 36 individual findings at roughly a 5:1 ratio, though comments 6 and 7 remain substantial in scope.\n\nLooking more closely, I can subdivide these further. Comment 6 breaks into fencing-specific items (four findings) and easement/boundary concerns (seven findings), suggesting a split between physical barriers and property line documentation. Comment 7 similarly contains distinct categories that might warrant separation.\n\nReconsidering the overlap—EL-1.25 about sidewalks actually fits better as a boundary/frontage element rather than pedestrian circulation, which shifts how I'm grouping these findings. This means I should reorganize the comments to reflect these clearer thematic boundaries and avoid redundancy across categories.\n\nThe revised structure consolidates findings into eight distinct comments: missing elevation sheets and section views for clearances, incorrect clearance annotations, unverified tree species, undocumented construction staging, incomplete fencing and sidewalk specifications, missing easement and property line documentation, and then site-level utility coordination issues. Each comment ties specific elevation items to a concrete deficiency, making the feedback more actionable.\n\nBefore detailing each comment further, I should map out where the overhead lines actually exist on the property—western, eastern, northern, and southern boundaries, with potential interior crossings—to ensure the documentation requirements address all relevant zones.\n\nThe site contains buildings clustered in the interior, surrounded by driveways, parking areas, landscaping, utility infrastructure, fencing, retaining walls, walkways, and a pond. This suggests organizing the clearance documentation by distinct areas: the four property boundaries where transmission lines appear, the interior site zone, and any missing plan elements. Looking at the specific findings, references to \"western property boundary where overhead electric lines are shown,\" \"Valley View Road\" frontage, and mentions of \"eastern and western\" conditions indicate the clearance gaps span multiple boundary conditions that need separate documentation. Let me reconsider the applicableArea assignments based on the guidance provided. The key is using short, canonical zone names that match the examples given. For the missing documentation comments, \"Plan Documentation\" fits since they're administrative issues about sheets and views. The fencing and boundary comments should map to specific frontages or \"Site-wide\" if they span multiple areas. Easement and utility conflicts belong in \"Utilities & Infrastructure\". Equipment placement could fall under \"Building Envelope\" if it's rooftop-related, or \"Site Interior\" for ground-level items. I need to check whether any comments reference the actual street names from the project facts to use the \"[Street Name] Frontage\" format where applicable. Comment 4 involves landscape features near property lines, so \"Site Interior\" fits. Comment 5 relates to construction documentation. For Comment 6, since it addresses fencing and boundaries, I should reference specific frontages where available—\"Valley View Road Frontage\" for the western side, and generic boundary designations like \"Eastern Boundary\" for the others lacking street names. The canonical examples show street-specific frontages like \"MoPac Expressway Frontage,\" suggesting I can name boundaries by their adjacent roads or cardinal directions.\n\nFor findings spanning multiple boundaries or the entire site, \"Site-wide\" works better than listing each boundary separately, given the 2-4 word constraint. So my categorization becomes: Comment 1 maps to \"Building Envelope,\" Comments 2 and 3 to \"Site-wide\" since they affect multiple features throughout, Comment 4 to \"Site Interior,\" Comment 5 to \"Plan Documentation,\" Comment 6 to either \"Utilities & Infrastructure\" or \"Site-wide\" depending on whether fencing is infrastructure-focused, and Comments 7 and 8 both to \"Utilities & Infrastructure\" for easements and site utilities.\n\nBut Comment 8 actually contains mixed elements—walkways and parking belong in \"Site Interior\" as circulation and site services, while water meters, transformers, and meter pads fit \"Utilities & Infrastructure.\" I should split this into two separate findings rather than forcing everything into one category.\n\nLooking at the breakdown: Site Interior covers EL-1.5, EL-1.19, EL-1.26, and EL-1.36, while Utilities & Infrastructure includes EL-1.28, EL-1.29, EL-1.38, and EL-1.43. Plan Documentation gets EL-1.33, and Building Envelope gets EL-1.39.\n\nSince the instructions require assigning each comment to a single applicable area, I need to handle multi-area comments differently. For those spanning multiple functional zones like Comments 6 and 8, \"Site-wide\" makes sense as the designation, or I could assign them to whichever area is most dominant. Let me reconsider whether splitting Comment 8 into two separate comments is the right approach, or if using \"Site-wide\" for comments affecting multiple areas is cleaner.\n\nRevising the assignments: Comment 1 goes to Building Envelope for the elevation sheets. Comment 2 becomes Site-wide since section views affect buildings, driveways, walls across different locations. Comment 3 is also Site-wide for clearance dimensions impacting multiple features. Comment 4 stays in Site Interior for tree species. Comment 5 belongs in Plan Documentation for construction staging. Comments 6, 7, and 8 all fit under Utilities & Infrastructure—fencing relates to boundary and OHE separation, easements address utility conflicts, and site equipment falls within infrastructure considerations.\n\nNow I should verify the regulatory citations before finalizing the full comment text. The findings reference several UCM sections: § 1.10.3 for building clearances, § 1.10.6.2 for vehicle clearances, § 1.14.0 for transmission clearances, § 1.10.10.4 for tree clearances, § 1.10.2 for construction clearances, and § 1.9.3 for additional requirements. be verified. The site plan lacks elevation views showing the vertical relationship between building elements and overhead conductors, making it impossible to confirm compliance with clearance requirements under UCM § 1.10.3.\n\nThe plan set contains only a 2D top-down view without any elevation sheets documenting surveyed conductor positions or clearance annotations. Registered surveyor data for conductor elevations is absent from the documentation.\n\nBuildings 1 and 2 sit approximately 25.8 to 26.1 feet from the western property boundary where overhead lines are located, but without conductor elevation data and building section views, vertical clearances cannot be confirmed. Building projections like overhangs and balconies also cannot be evaluated for adequate clearance from the overhead conductors without the necessary elevation documentation. Let me reconsider how to handle the detail text. The instruction is clear: extract the most specific observations from all run comments, preserve concrete data points, and cut unnecessary framing.\n\nFor EL-1.7, the three runs all identify the same core issue—missing elevation sheets—but contain distinct details worth capturing. Run 2 specifies that the site plan is limited to 2D top-down views with no vertical perspectives, while Run 3 notes the absence of registered surveyor data for conductor elevations. Merging these: \"No building elevation sheets showing surveyed overhead conductor elevations and clearance annotations are provided. Site plan is 2D-only; registered surveyor data for conductor elevations is absent.\"\n\nFor EL-1.1, the runs describe buildings positioned 25.8-26.1 feet from the western property boundary where overhead lines exist, but lack surveyed conductor elevations and clearance dimensions. The merged text should capture the specific distance and the verification gap: \"Buildings 1-2 located 25.8-26.1 feet from western property boundary (where OHE lines exist), but no surveyed conductor elevations or clearance dimensions are provided. Sky-to-ground clearance cannot be verified.\"\n\nFor EL-1.3, both runs flag the absence of elevation sheets and the inability to assess building projections against conductor clearances. The merged detail should note: \"No building elevation sheets provided. Building projections (overhangs, awnings, balconies) cannot be evaluated for clearances from overhead conductors without elevation documentation and conductor survey data.\"\n\nNow I'm organizing the full JSON structure with 8 comments total. Each comment needs a headline, summary, applicable sheets, area, severity, and confidence level. The details array will contain the specific findings with citations and run-level comments showing whether each run failed or couldn't be verified. I'll structure the runComments to preserve the verbatim observations from each run while tracking confidence based on how many runs flagged the same issue. I need to compile the sheet references across all three checklist items for this comment. EL-1.7 pulls sheets [1, 9, 4, 11], EL-1.1 references [4, 9], and EL-1.3 adds [21], giving me a deduplicated set of [1, 4, 9, 11, 21]. The summary should concisely capture the core deficiency—that required building elevation documentation showing overhead conductor clearances is absent—while keeping the detail entries focused on specific checklist findings rather than repeating the summary content. Building elevation sheets with registered surveyor conductor elevation data and clearance annotations are entirely absent from the plan set. The site plan provides only 2D top-down views without any vertical elevation documentation needed to verify clearances.\n\nBuildings 1-2 are positioned 25.8-26.1 feet from the western property boundary where overhead lines are present, yet no surveyed conductor elevations or clearance dimensions have been documented to confirm adequate separation. status\": \"nv\",\n          \"comment\": \"Building elevation sheets are absent from the submitted site plan documentation. Without these sheets and surveyor-provided conductor elevation data, I cannot verify clearances between building projections such as overhangs, awnings, and balconies and the overhead electric conductors.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n**Comment 2 - Missing Section Views for Vertical Clearances**\n\nSeveral code sections require section views demonstrating vertical clearances to overhead conductors: EL-1.8 addresses buildings and driveways, EL-1.18 covers driveways specifically, EL-1.20 applies to transmission lines and roads, EL-1.14 concerns retaining walls, and EL-1.17 requires retaining wall sections with conductor clearance dimensions. Each of these needs corresponding section views. Let me consolidate these findings:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"headline\": \"Missing section views showing vertical overhead conductor clearances\",\n  \"summary\n\n\": \"Section and profile views with vertical clearance dimension annotations from outside conductors to buildings, driveways, roads, and retaining walls are not provided. Vertical clearance compliance cannot be documented.\",\n  \"sheets\": [9, 11, 38, 39],\n  \"applicableArea\": \"Site-wide\",\n  \"severity\": 3,\n  \"confidence\": 3,\n  \"crossDep\": \"\",\n  \"attn\": \"\",\n  \"details\": [\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Site plan shows buildings and overhead electric lines but no section views or enlarged details with dimension lines from outside conductor to building edges are provided.\",\n      \"citation\": \"UCM § 1.10.3\",\n      \"sourceId\": \"1:EL-1.8\",\n      \"confidence\": 3,\n      \"runComments\": [\n        {\n          \"runNum\": 1,\n          \"status\": \"fail\",\n          \"comment\": \"Site plan shows buildings and overhead electric lines but no section views or enlarged details with clearance dimensions from outside conductor to building edges are provided.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"runNum\": 2,\n          \"status\": \"nv\",\n          \"comment\": \"No section or profile view exhibits provided showing dimension lines from outer conductor to building edges. Horizontal clearance dimensions shown on plan views alone are insufficient without vertical section documentation.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n``` The grading plan shows driveway and parking areas with spot elevations, but lacks cross-section views that would document vertical clearance from finished grades to the lowest points of overhead conductors. Surveyed conductor elevations and mid-span clearance data are not provided in the submittal.\n\nTransmission line crossings are noted on the drawings, but the 30-foot minimum vertical clearance requirement is not demonstrated through section views or profile details that compare finished driveway grades to transmission line heights.\n\nThe retaining walls reaching 14 feet in height lack required section views showing clearance dimensions from the electric conductors to the wall surfaces, which is a critical omission for compliance verification. but clearance dimensions from the outside conductor to wall faces aren't documented on the site plan or sections. The walls run parallel to the overhead electric line along the southern boundary, yet specific clearance measurements from the outer conductor to the wall faces are absent from the drawings.\n\nThis observation connects several related findings across the electrical plan—missing dimension annotations from conductors rather than poles, absent dimensions for customer facilities and buildings, and lack of surveyor verification. All point to a systemic gap where required clearance dimensions either aren't provided or reference incorrect measurement points.\n\nThe core issue is that clearance dimensions shown on the site plan (26.1', 29.6', 21.3') lack verifiable origin points—they may originate from pole centerline instead of the outside conductor surface as mandated by code. Without confirmation that measurements follow UCM § 1.10.3, compliance cannot be established. The dimension annotations need to explicitly originate from the actual conductor position to satisfy code requirements. The overhead distribution lines visible on the site plan lack radial distance dimensions to customer facilities like transformers, pull boxes, and electrical meters. Additionally, the voltage classification of these overhead conductors isn't documented, which prevents verification of required clearance compliance.\n\nBuildings positioned at property boundaries adjacent to overhead electric infrastructure in the right-of-way don't show clearance measurements from the outside conductor on the site plan, creating another documentation gap for code compliance verification. \"comment\": \"Registered surveyor documentation of mid-span clearances, pole positions, and conductor heights from overhead transmission lines to proposed driveways, parking, and retaining walls is missing. The electrical infrastructure survey required to verify adequate vertical clearance has not been completed.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"runNum\": 3,\n          \"status\": \"fail\",\n          \"comment\": \"Multiple overhead electric lines border the site with proposed improvements—driveways, parking areas, and retaining walls—positioned near these facilities. Pole locations, mid-span clearances, and conductor elevations have not been surveyed by a registered professional. Complete electrical clearance documentation is absent from the submission.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n**Comment 4 - Proposed Trees Not Verified as Utility-Compatible Species**\n\nThis is EL-1.37 only.\n\nProposed tree plantings near overhead transmission lines lack species identification and haven't been cross-checked against ECM Appendix F's Utility Compatible (UC) requirements, making compliance verification impossible. Trees throughout the site—particularly those near overhead lines on the western, northern, and southern boundaries—are not identified by species or referenced against the UC list. Mature height must not exceed 20 feet for any trees within 50 lateral feet of overhead transmission conductors. the landscape plan fails to cross-reference proposed species against the utility-compatible list in ECM Appendix F, and there are no annotations indicating clearance distances from trees to the overhead conductors on the western, northern, and southern boundaries. Without species verification, I cannot confirm compliance with the tree height restrictions for areas within 50 lateral feet of transmission lines.\n\nThe construction staging plan lacks documentation of equipment clearances and work zones relative to overhead transmission lines. A 10-foot radial clearance requirement during construction operations is not addressed in the submitted plans, preventing verification of construction-phase electrical safety compliance. per Sheet 3 Electric Transmission Notes item #3). The documentation doesn't verify that staging areas maintain the required clearance from overhead lines.\n\nThe construction sequence appears on Sheet 3, and temporary erosion controls are shown on Sheet 5, but there's no detailed staging plan that maps equipment placement, crane swing radius, and material storage locations or confirms they stay 10 feet away from the overhead transmission lines. Fencing and boundary features also lack documented clearance verification from the electrical infrastructure. overhead lines, but no dimension annotations show clearance distance from outside conductor. Clearance verification not provided. The site plan depicts fencing at the pond perimeter and along property boundaries, yet lacks documented clearance dimensions from the nearest overhead conductors, making compliance verification impossible.\n\nAdditionally, utility pole and fence post locations appear on the survey and site plan without corresponding horizontal dimensions between these elements. The spacing between fence posts and pole centerlines remains undocumented, preventing verification of adequate separation.\n\nFencing is proposed near transmission facilities on the site, but material specifications—whether conductive or non-conductive—and grounding details are absent from the construction details. The documentation doesn't clarify the relationship between the proposed fencing and existing transmission lines or provide the necessary specifications for safe installation.\n\nA pond perimeter fence is located adjacent to an overhead electric line, yet no section details document the required vertical and horizontal clearance distances between the fence and the electric line. Clearance verification is missing entirely.\n\nProposed sidewalks along the western property boundary (Valley View Road) intersect with existing utility poles shown near the property corners. The drawings don't dimension or verify how the sidewalk alignment relates to these pole locations, leaving potential conflicts unresolved. show line crossing orientation. Line crossing angles are not documented or analyzed for conflicts with road geometry or sight lines.\",\n      \"citation\": \"Reviewer Convention\",\n      \"sourceId\": \"1:EL-1.32\",\n      \"confidence\": 1,\n      \"runComments\": [\n        {\n          \"runNum\": 1,\n          \"status\": \"nv\",\n          \"comment\": \"Overhead line crossings of Valley View Road are not documented with angle annotations. Line crossing angles are not documented or analyzed for conflicts with road geometry or sight lines.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Down guy wire locations and anchor points not shown on site plan. Existing down guy wires on adjacent properties not identified or analyzed for conflicts with proposed site improvements.\",\n      \"citation\": \"Reviewer Convention\",\n      \"sourceId\": \"1:EL-1.40\",\n      \"confidence\": 2,\n      \"runComments\": [\n        {\n          \"runNum\": 1,\n          \"status\": \"nv\",\n          \"comment\": \"Down guy wire locations and anchor points are not shown on the site plan. Existing down guy wires on adjacent properties are not identified or analyzed for conflicts with proposed site improvements.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Guy wire easement boundaries not documented on site plan or in project narrative. Easement limits and maintenance access requirements not addressed.\",\n      \"citation\": \"Reviewer Convention\",\n      \"sourceId\": \"1:EL-1.41\",\n      \"confidence\": 2,\n      \"runComments\": [\n        {\n          \"runNum\": 1,\n          \"status\": \"nv\",\n          \"comment\": \"Guy wire easement boundaries are not documented on the site plan or in the project narrative. Easement limits and maintenance access requirements are not addressed.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Down guy anchor locations on adjacent properties not identified or verified. Anchor easement boundaries and property access for maintenance not documented.\",\n      \"citation\": \"Reviewer Convention\",\n      \"sourceId\": \"1:EL-1.42\",\n      \"confidence\": 2,\n      \"runComments\": [\n        {\n          \"runNum\": 1,\n          \"status\": \"nv\",\n          \"comment\": \"Down guy anchor locations on adjacent properties are not identified or verified. Anchor easement boundaries and property access for maintenance are not documented.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nThe overhead line crossing angles need documentation showing how the lines intersect Valley View Road—this is critical for sight line analysis and road safety. Down guy wires and their anchor points are completely absent from the site plan, which creates potential conflicts with the proposed improvements and adjacent properties. The guy wire easement boundaries should be clearly marked and their maintenance access requirements specified. Building locations need verification against transmission easement restrictions, and foundation clearances from property lines must be confirmed to ensure compliance with adjacent utility facilities. All of these elements—easement boundaries, line crossing angles, guy wire locations, and property line clearances—need to be documented and analyzed together as an integrated utility infrastructure assessment. 1, with comments noting that transmission facilities are present but guy easement boundaries (10' wide ± 50' extension) aren't explicitly marked on the easement exhibit or site plan. The review identifies several utility infrastructure gaps: overhead line crossings lack angle annotations to confirm 90-degree orientation; utility pole locations are documented but down guy wire positions aren't clearly shown, preventing conflict verification with proposed site features; and required easement documentation for down guy anchors (10-foot-wide extending 5 feet past ground entry points) is missing from the plat sheet despite transmission facilities affecting the property. ramps shown on site plans without section views demonstrating 15-foot radial clearance from outside conductors. Conductor elevations not surveyed. Parking areas lack clearance verification documentation, with no cross-sections showing distances from overhead lines. Trash enclosures, water utility connections, site lighting fixtures, and electrical equipment similarly lack dimensional clearance annotations. Meter pad specifications missing conductor type information and required working space dimensions per code. areas are shown but no documentation addresses whether they are beneath overhead lines or whether trash bin service clearances have been verified. Trash collection is addressed in site plan notes, and enclosure locations are identified on the plan, but clearance verification from receptacle locations to overhead electric lines remains undocumented, as do raised height considerations during service operations.\n\nWater meter locations appear on the electrical design plan, though no dimensions indicate clearance distances from meter locations to overhead electric service drops. runNum\": 3,\n          \"status\": \"nv\",\n          \"comment\": \"Overhead and underground electrical infrastructure is depicted on both site and electrical design plans, yet the distinction between primary and secondary line types and how they interact at crossing points remains undocumented.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Electrical design plan lacks documentation of conductor separation distances and clearance verification from proposed equipment locations to existing overhead electric service infrastructure.\",\n      \"citation\": \"Reviewer Convention\",\n      \"sourceId\": \"1:EL-1.35\",\n      \"confidence\": 2,\n      \"runComments\": [\n        {\n          \"runNum\": 1,\n          \"status\": \"nv\",\n          \"comment\": \"Electrical design plan does not show separation distances between proposed equipment and overhead service conductors.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"runNum\": 3,\n          \"status\": \"nv\",\n          \"comment\": \"No clearance measurements are provided between equipment placement areas and overhead electrical infrastructure on the design documentation.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    } clearances (30-inch-wide front space, 36-inch front depth, 6.5-foot headroom) are maintained free from obstructions. The design documentation lacks the detailed verification needed to confirm compliance with clearance requirements.\n\nThe electrical plan identifies streetlight pole locations near Valley View Road, but doesn't provide dimensions showing the separation between proposed buildings and existing poles.\n\nThe transformer locations are depicted on the electrical design plan relative to overhead lines, though section views with vertical clearance measurements from overhead conductors to transformer tops are missing.\n\nNow I'm consolidating the findings into the final JSON structure. I have 8 distinct comments compiled, with the first grouping 3 findings together, the second combining 5 findings, the third consolidating 4 findings, and subsequent comments containing 1, 1, and 5 findings respectively.\n\nThe remaining comments group together 6 and 10 findings, with one finding excluded from the consolidation. This accounts for all 36 findings across the 8 comment blocks, yielding a consolidation ratio of 4.5 findings per comment, which seems reasonable for the scope of this audit.","signature":""}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cbf6244c-82ca-4498-85ed-9099636fbccb","uuid":"9bacf711-6669-488e-baf3-2ca468b20087"}
{"level":30,"time":1778246851359,"pid":122,"hostname":"4de9b0d3-b46","step":"synthesize-comments","item":"1.json","index":3,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR3W60KQ17VB1KA6QWBBJ6E8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I'll synthesize these electrical utility findings into well-structured review comments, consolidating the 36 applicable findings (excluding EL-1.21, which applies to highway grading not present at this site) into approximately 8 comments with meaningful consolidation.\n\n**Discipline Scope:** Utility (Electrical) — overhead electric facilities, clearances, transmission easements, and conductor survey data.\n\n**Consolidation Strategy:** Findings cluster around missing plan documentation (elevation sheets, section views), missing dimension annotations, missing surveyor data, and missing feature-specific clearance documentation. I'm merging aggressively by regulatory requirement and corrective action, targeting ~4.5:1 consolidation."}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cbf6244c-82ca-4498-85ed-9099636fbccb","uuid":"95e986a0-2955-4edd-ba3f-946cacfccb83"}
{"level":30,"time":1778246892688,"pid":122,"hostname":"4de9b0d3-b46","step":"synthesize-comments","item":"2.json","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR3W60M5TWM0NB7WW9JAMX56","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0173eU5B5E3Tri4KUfAushzN","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"comments":[{"headline":"Overhead distribution clearance violations and missing Austin Energy approval","summary":"Trees occupy prohibited clearance zones from the overhead distribution line and utility poles. Run 1 confirmed violations for non-utility-compatible species and pole proximity. However, species identification, mature height documentation, and clearance dimensions are missing, hindering full verification. No documented Austin Energy approval exists for trees in restricted zones.","sheets":[31,35],"applicableArea":"Overhead electric distribution - southern boundary","severity":3,"confidence":2,"crossDep":"Austin Energy (for approval of restricted-zone tree placement)","attn":"Clarify which trees are proposed in restricted zones. If trees remain in restricted zones, obtain written Austin Energy approval.","details":[{"text":"Non-utility-compatible Live Oak (not designated UC in ECM Appendix F) is proposed within 15 lateral feet of the overhead distribution conductor. Tree must be relocated beyond 15 feet or documented Austin Energy approval provided.","citation":"UCM § 1.10.10.4","sourceId":"2:EL-2.1","confidence":3,"runComments":[{"runNum":1,"status":"fail","comment":"Non-utility-compatible trees (Live Oak) are proposed within 15 lateral feet of the overhead electric distribution line. No clearance dimensions or Austin Energy approvals are provided. Live Oak (Quercus virginiana) is not marked as utility-compatible in ECM Appendix F and is therefore prohibited within 15 feet of distribution conductors. Applicant must either relocate the Live Oak trees beyond 15 feet of the OHE line or provide documented Austin Energy written approval for the exception."},{"runNum":2,"status":"nv","comment":"Trees marked as 'M' (Mitigation) and 'P' (Parking Buffer) are positioned 5-10 feet from the overhead electric (OHE) line on the landscape plan, which falls within the < 15 lateral feet distribution restriction zone requiring utility-compatible species only. However, the landscape plan does not identify which specific species are labeled with these symbols, and the plant list on Sheet 35 does not indicate which of the four proposed shade/ornamental species (Cedar Elm, Live Oak, Texas Mountain Laurel, Mexican Redbud) are the 'M' and 'P' trees. Without species identification linked to tree symbol locations, and without ECM Appendix F utility-compatible designations, compliance cannot be verified."},{"runNum":3,"status":"nv","comment":"Overhead electric distribution line (OHE) is present on the site. Proposed tree species are listed on Sheet 35 (Cedar Elm, Live Oak, Mexican Redbud, Texas Mountain Laurel), but utility-compatible (UC) status cannot be verified from submitted documents as ECM Appendix F is not included. Additionally, no clearance dimensions are shown on the landscape plan to verify whether any non-utility-compatible trees are within the required 15-foot lateral distance from the distribution conductor."}]},{"text":"Large trees (mature height 40+ feet, such as Live Oak) are proposed within 25 lateral feet of overhead distribution conductors. Tree must be relocated beyond 25 feet or documented Austin Energy approval provided.","citation":"UCM § 1.10.10.4","sourceId":"2:EL-2.3","confidence":3,"runComments":[{"runNum":1,"status":"fail","comment":"Large trees (Live Oak, mature height 40+ feet) are proposed within 25 lateral feet of overhead electric distribution conductors. No clearance dimensions, spacing requirements, or Austin Energy written approval are documented. Applicant must either relocate the Live Oak trees to greater than 25 feet from the distribution conductors or provide documented Austin Energy written approval for the exception."},{"runNum":2,"status":"nv","comment":"Trees are positioned within 25 feet of the overhead electric line. The requirement prohibits large trees (mature height 40+ feet per ECM Appendix F) within this zone. However, the landscape plan does not differentiate which trees are 'large trees' (40+ feet mature height). Sheet 35 lists species with their container size specifications but does not document mature heights per ECM Appendix F. Without mature height documentation linked to tree locations on the plan, compliance cannot be verified."},{"runNum":3,"status":"nv","comment":"Proposed tree species list does not include mature height specifications. Sheet 35 provides minimum container sizes (100 gal., 45 gal.) and caliper inches (4\", 2.5\") but not the mature heights required to determine which trees are 'large trees' (40+ feet per ECM Appendix F). Without mature height data and clearance dimensions, compliance with the 25-foot distribution clearance requirement for large trees cannot be verified."}]},{"text":"Trees are proposed within 10 lateral feet of utility poles on the overhead electric line. At least one mitigation tree on the southeastern boundary is positioned within approximately 10 feet of a utility pole. Tree must be relocated beyond 10 feet (measured from trunk center to pole structure).","citation":"UCM § 1.10.10.4","sourceId":"2:EL-2.6","confidence":3,"runComments":[{"runNum":1,"status":"fail","comment":"Trees are proposed within 10 lateral feet of overhead electric utility poles. At least one mitigation tree shown on the landscape plan is positioned within approximately 10 feet of a utility pole on the southeastern property boundary. The tree must be relocated to greater than 10 feet from the pole, measured from tree trunk center to pole structure."},{"runNum":2,"status":"nv","comment":"Overhead electric poles are shown on the landscape plan as circular symbols with 'x' marks along the OHE line on the southern boundary. Trees marked 'M' and 'P' are positioned 5-10 feet from the OHE line. The requirement prohibits trees within 10 lateral feet of utility poles. However, the landscape plan does not provide exact dimensions from tree trunk centers to pole centerlines, and the 'M' and 'P' tree species are not identified on the plan. Without exact measurements and species identification, compliance cannot be verified."},{"runNum":3,"status":"nv","comment":"Utility pole symbols are shown on the landscape plan (Sheet 31) and site plan (Sheet 9) along the OHE line, but their exact locations are not dimensioned. Proposed tree locations are shown but no clearance dimensions are provided from trees to the poles. Therefore, the 10-foot lateral clearance requirement cannot be verified."}]},{"text":"No documented written approval from Austin Energy has been provided for trees proposed in prohibited clearance zones (within 15 feet of distribution conductors, within 25 feet for large trees, within 10 feet of utility poles).","citation":"UCM § 1.10.10.4","sourceId":"2:EL-2.15","confidence":2,"runComments":[{"runNum":1,"status":"fail","comment":"Trees are proposed within prohibited clearance zones from overhead electric facilities (within 15 feet of distribution conductors, within 25 feet for large trees, and within 10 feet of utility poles) without documented written approval from Austin Energy. Applicant must either relocate the violating trees outside restricted zones or provide explicit written approval from Austin Energy authorizing the exception. Letters or approvals should be attached to the site plan or submitted as supplementary documentation."},{"runNum":2,"status":"nv","comment":"Trees are positioned within restricted clearance zones (5-10 feet from the OHE line and utility poles along the southern boundary). The plans do not provide evidence of Austin Energy written approval for these placements. No approval letters, correspondence, or notes in the submitted site plan documents indicate that Austin Energy has authorized the tree placement in these restricted zones. If trees within prohibited zones are proposed, documented written approval from Austin Energy must be provided on or attached to the site plan."},{"runNum":3,"status":"nv","comment":"Cannot determine whether any trees are proposed within prohibited clearance zones without: (1) species identification with UC status verification against ECM Appendix F, (2) mature height identification to determine if trees are large (40+ feet), (3) clearance dimensions from trees to conductors and equipment. Therefore, it cannot be determined whether Austin Energy written approval is required."}]}]},{"headline":"Tree species and mature height documentation missing; clearance dimensions not shown","summary":"Tree symbols on the landscape plan show only functional labels (M, P, SY, PL) without species identification or mature heights. Plant schedule lists species but lacks mature height documentation per ECM Appendix F and species-to-location cross-reference. Clearance dimensions from trees to electrical facilities are not shown. Without this documentation, utility-compatibility and large-tree distinction (40+ feet) cannot be verified.","sheets":[31,35,21],"applicableArea":"Landscape plan - site-wide tree documentation","severity":2,"confidence":2,"crossDep":"","attn":"Provide clear plant legend on landscape plan linking tree symbols to species names and mature heights per ECM Appendix F. Add dimension lines from tree trunk centers to overhead electric line, utility poles, and underground equipment locations.","details":[{"text":"Landscape plan labels trees with functional designations ('M', 'P', 'SY', 'PL') but does not identify botanical or common names. Plant list on Sheet 35 specifies four species (Cedar Elm, Live Oak, Texas Mountain Laurel, Mexican Redbud) but does not cross-reference which species correspond to which symbols on the drawing. Reviewer must manually correlate species to locations across multiple sheets.","citation":"Reviewer Convention","sourceId":"2:EL-2.10","confidence":2,"runComments":[{"runNum":2,"status":"fail","comment":"Trees proposed near the overhead electric line on the landscape plan are labeled with functional designations ('M' for Mitigation, 'P' for Parking Buffer) but the landscape plan does not directly identify the botanical or common names of these specific trees. The plant list on Sheet 35 specifies species (Cedar Elm, Live Oak, Texas Mountain Laurel, Mexican Redbud) but does not cross-reference which species are labeled 'M' or 'P' on the actual drawing. A reviewer must reference multiple sheets and cannot directly correlate tree symbols on the landscape plan to specific species. Provide a clear plant legend on the landscape plan that identifies the species of each labeled tree, or clearly reference the plant schedule with species names linked to drawing symbols."},{"runNum":3,"status":"fail","comment":"The landscape plan (Sheet 31) uses abbreviation codes to label tree locations (M = Mitigation Tree, SY = Street Yard Tree, PL = Parking Lot Tree, P = Parking Buffer Point Tree) but provides no legend on that sheet correlating these codes to the specific botanical names. The plant schedule with species identification is located on a separate sheet (Sheet 35). A reader cannot determine which species is planted at which location on the landscape plan without cross-referencing multiple sheets."}]},{"text":"Landscape plan does not differentiate large trees (mature height 40+ feet per ECM Appendix F) from smaller species through notation, color-coding, legend, or labeling. Plant schedule on Sheet 35 provides container specifications and caliper sizes but does not document mature heights for any proposed species.","citation":"Reviewer Convention","sourceId":"2:EL-2.11","confidence":3,"runComments":[{"runNum":1,"status":"fail","comment":"Large trees (40+ feet mature height) are not visually differentiated on the landscape plan. All tree symbols appear identical regardless of mature height. Applicant should modify the landscape plan to specifically label or differentiate large trees (such as Live Oak at 40+ feet) to facilitate verification of clearances from electric facilities. This can be accomplished through unique symbols, labels, or callout notes on the landscape plan."},{"runNum":2,"status":"fail","comment":"The landscape plan does not differentiate or designate large trees (mature height 40+ feet per ECM Appendix F) from smaller trees. Tree symbols on the plan are marked by functional category ('M', 'P', 'SY', 'PL') but include no notation, color-coding, or legend entry identifying which trees are 'large' (≥40 feet mature height). The plant schedule on Sheet 35 does not document mature heights per ECM Appendix F. Large trees positioned within 25 feet of overhead distribution facilities require specific clearance compliance; without identification of large trees on the plan, such compliance cannot be verified. Provide a clear designation (notation, color, legend symbol) on the landscape plan identifying trees with mature heights of 40 feet or greater per ECM Appendix F."},{"runNum":3,"status":"fail","comment":"The landscape plan does not indicate mature heights for any of the proposed trees. Sheet 35 provides minimum container specifications and caliper sizes but does not specify the mature height for any tree species. Without mature height information on or cross-referenced to the landscape plan, large trees (those with mature height 40+ feet per ECM Appendix F) cannot be differentiated from smaller trees, making it impossible to verify clearance compliance for large trees from overhead electric facilities."}]},{"text":"Landscape plan lacks dimension lines or measurements from tree trunk centers to the overhead electric line, utility poles, or between conductors and pole centerlines. Without dimensional evidence, the measurement method (pole centerline versus outside conductor position) cannot be verified.","citation":"UCM § 1.10.10.4","sourceId":"2:EL-2.12","confidence":2,"runComments":[{"runNum":2,"status":"nv","comment":"The landscape plan does not provide dimension lines or measurements from tree trunk centers to the overhead electric line or from the OHE line to utility poles. While pole symbols are shown on the OHE line, no dimensions or calculations are provided to demonstrate that clearances are measured from the outside conductor (not pole centerline). Without dimensional evidence on the plan, the measurement methodology cannot be verified."},{"runNum":3,"status":"nv","comment":"No clearance dimensions are shown on the landscape plan from trees to the overhead electric conductor or poles. Because no dimensions are present, the method of measurement (whether from pole centerline versus conductor, whether from trunk center versus canopy edge) cannot be evaluated. If clearance dimensions were to be added to the plan, they must originate from the outside conductor position, not pole centerline."}]},{"text":"No dimension lines or measurements from tree locations are provided on the landscape plan. Without dimensional documentation, it cannot be determined whether any distance measurements (if shown elsewhere) originate from tree trunk centers (correct per requirement) or projected canopy edges (incorrect).","citation":"UCM § 1.10.10.4","sourceId":"2:EL-2.13","confidence":2,"runComments":[{"runNum":2,"status":"nv","comment":"The landscape plan does not provide dimension lines or measurements from tree locations. Without dimensioned evidence showing whether measurements originate at tree trunk centers (planting locations) or at projected canopy edges, the measurement methodology cannot be verified."},{"runNum":3,"status":"nv","comment":"No clearance dimensions are shown on the landscape plan. Because dimensions are absent, it cannot be determined whether any dimensions that may exist elsewhere originate from tree trunk centers (correct) or tree canopy edges (incorrect). Proper clearance measurement must originate from tree trunk center (planting location)."}]},{"text":"Landscape plan (Sheet 31) and electrical plan (Sheet 21) do not clearly cross-reference or align. Reviewer must manually verify that all overhead conductors and poles shown on the electrical plan are depicted on the landscape plan relative to proposed tree locations.","citation":"Reviewer Convention","sourceId":"2:EL-2.14","confidence":2,"runComments":[{"runNum":2,"status":"nv","comment":"The landscape plan (Sheet 31) shows the overhead electric (OHE) line and poles along the southern boundary. However, the electrical plan (Sheet 21) shows only underground electrical infrastructure; the two plans do not clearly cross-reference or align. While the OHE line is present on the landscape plan, a reviewer must cross-reference between sheets to verify that all overhead conductors and poles are depicted on the landscape plan. The electrical plan should be referenced or overlaid on the landscape plan to provide complete clarity on overhead facility locations and ensure all conductors are shown relative to tree locations."},{"runNum":3,"status":"fail","comment":"While the overhead electric line (OHE) is shown on the landscape plan and tree symbols are present, the plans do not provide sufficient information to verify clearances. Specifically: (1) no dimension lines or distance measurements are provided from trees to the OHE conductor; (2) the tree symbols on the landscape plan are not identified by species (only by functional category: M, SY, PL), so utility-compatibility cannot be assessed; (3) utility pole locations are shown graphically but not dimensioned. The lack of dimensional cross-reference and species identification prevents verification that trees comply with required clearance distances from overhead electric facilities."}]}]},{"headline":"Underground electrical equipment clearances and root barriers not documented","summary":"Electrical design plan shows underground equipment but does not distinguish pad-mounted items. Landscape plan lacks overlay of underground equipment locations with trees, preventing verification of 5-foot clearance requirements. Root barrier specifications (4 feet deep, 5 feet from equipment) for trees within 20 feet are not documented.","sheets":[21,31],"applicableArea":"Underground electrical equipment - Site Interior","severity":2,"confidence":2,"crossDep":"Electrical design (coordinate sheet overlays and equipment identification)","attn":"Clarify pad-mounted equipment locations. Overlay electrical plan with landscape plan. Specify root barrier details for trees within 20 feet of underground equipment.","details":[{"text":"Electrical design plan does not clearly distinguish pad-mounted equipment from other electrical elements (transformers, pull boxes, meters). Landscape plan does not show electrical equipment locations in relation to proposed trees. Compliance with 10-foot clearance requirement from pad-mounted equipment cannot be verified.","citation":"UCM § 1.10.10.4","sourceId":"2:EL-2.7","confidence":1,"runComments":[{"runNum":3,"status":"nv","comment":"Electrical design plan (Sheet 21) shows underground electrical infrastructure (transformers, pull boxes, electrical meters) but does not clearly distinguish pad-mounted equipment from other electrical elements. The landscape plan does not show electrical equipment locations in relation to proposed trees. Therefore, compliance with the 10-foot clearance requirement from pad-mounted equipment cannot be verified."}]},{"text":"Underground electrical equipment locations (per Sheet 21) are not clearly indicated on the landscape plan in relation to proposed trees. Electrical design plan and landscape plan are not overlaid or cross-referenced, preventing verification that trees maintain required 5-foot distance from underground equipment.","citation":"UCM § 1.10.10.4","sourceId":"2:EL-2.8","confidence":2,"runComments":[{"runNum":1,"status":"nv","comment":"Underground electric equipment locations are not clearly indicated on the landscape plan in relation to proposed trees. Applicant should show underground equipment locations and identify any trees within 5 lateral feet of such equipment."},{"runNum":3,"status":"nv","comment":"Electrical design plan (Sheet 21) shows underground electrical elements but the landscape plan (Sheet 31) does not overlay electrical equipment locations with proposed tree locations. Clearance dimensions from trees to underground equipment are not shown. The 5-foot lateral clearance requirement cannot be verified from the submitted plans."}]},{"text":"Root barrier specifications (minimum 4 feet deep, positioned 5 feet from equipment) required for trees within 20 feet of underground electrical equipment are not shown on the landscape plan. Clearance dimensions from trees to underground equipment are absent, preventing determination of whether any trees fall within the 20-foot threshold requiring root barriers.","citation":"UCM § 1.10.10.4","sourceId":"2:EL-2.9","confidence":2,"runComments":[{"runNum":1,"status":"nv","comment":"Cannot verify whether root barriers are specified for trees within 20 lateral feet of underground electric equipment. Applicant should show underground equipment locations on the landscape plan and specify root barrier requirements (minimum 4 feet deep, positioned 5 feet from equipment) for any trees within 20 feet."},{"runNum":3,"status":"nv","comment":"While underground electrical equipment is shown on Sheet 21, the landscape plan does not provide root barrier specifications or details. No clearance dimensions are shown from trees to underground equipment to determine whether any trees fall within the 20-foot threshold requiring root barriers. Root barrier requirements therefore cannot be verified."}]}]},{"headline":"Transmission infrastructure and easement boundaries not identified on plans","summary":"Landscape plan does not identify transmission facilities, locations, or easement boundaries, preventing verification of 50-foot transmission conductor clearances and easement tree prohibitions. Applicant must clarify whether transmission infrastructure is present and, if so, depict all transmission lines, structures, and easement boundaries on landscape and grading plans.","sheets":[31,2],"applicableArea":"Transmission easement areas (if present) - Site-wide","severity":2,"confidence":1,"crossDep":"","attn":"Confirm whether transmission infrastructure is present on the property. If present, provide transmission easement boundaries, structure locations, and clearance dimensions on all applicable sheets.","details":[{"text":"Transmission facilities, easement locations, and structure positions are not clearly identified on the landscape plan. Verification of compliance with 50-foot transmission conductor clearance requirements cannot be completed without transmission infrastructure locations depicted.","citation":"UCM § 1.10.10.4","sourceId":"2:EL-2.2","confidence":1,"runComments":[{"runNum":1,"status":"nv","comment":"Cannot verify whether transmission facilities are present or the location and extent of transmission easements. The landscape plan does not clearly identify transmission infrastructure or easement boundaries, preventing verification of compliance with 50-foot transmission conductor clearance requirements. Applicant should clarify whether transmission facilities are present and, if so, show transmission easement boundaries and structure locations on the landscape plan."}]},{"text":"Large trees (40+ feet mature height) positioned within 50 lateral feet of transmission conductors cannot be verified without identification and location of transmission infrastructure. Transmission facility locations are not shown on the landscape plan.","citation":"UCM § 1.10.10.4","sourceId":"2:EL-2.4","confidence":1,"runComments":[{"runNum":1,"status":"nv","comment":"Cannot verify whether large trees (40+ feet) are positioned within 50 lateral feet of transmission conductors due to unclear identification of transmission infrastructure and easement boundaries on the landscape plan. Applicant should clarify transmission facility locations if present."}]},{"text":"Transmission easement boundaries are not depicted on the landscape plan, grading plan, or plat. Trees cannot be verified as positioned outside transmission easements without clear delineation of easement areas. If transmission facilities are present, easement boundaries must be shown on all relevant plan sheets per UCM § 1.14.6.","citation":"UCM § 1.10.10.4; UCM § 1.14.6","sourceId":"2:EL-2.5","confidence":1,"runComments":[{"runNum":1,"status":"nv","comment":"Transmission easement boundaries are not clearly shown on the landscape plan, grading plan, or plat. Without depicted easement areas, it cannot be verified that trees are positioned outside transmission easements. Applicant must show transmission easement boundaries on all relevant plan sheets if transmission facilities are present on the property."}]}]}]}}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2f050a35-57ac-4146-a5ef-421177d94112","uuid":"7557a304-b96e-4049-8e10-1c8e6863d8a7"}
